{"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"patch":0,"minor":3},"abstract":[{"text":"The sound element contains general playback parameters. They can stand alone within a part\/measure, or be a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"component element within a direction.","type":"text"}],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLSound"},"sections":[],"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"platforms":["macOS"],"languages":["swift"],"tokens":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLSound","kind":"identifier"}]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"kind":"content","content":[{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Overview","anchor":"overview"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Tempo is expressed in quarter notes per minute. If 0, the sound-generating program should prompt the user at","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the time of compiling a sound (MIDI) file.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Dynamics (or MIDI velocity) are expressed as a percentage of the default forte value (90 for MIDI 1.0).","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Dacapo indicates to go back to the beginning of the movement. When used it always has the value “yes”.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Segno and dalsegno are used for backwards jumps to a segno sign; coda and tocoda are used for forward jumps to","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"a coda sign. If there are multiple jumps, the value of these parameters can be used to name and distinguish","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"them. If segno or coda is used, the divisions attribute can also be used to indicate the number of divisions","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"per quarter note. Otherwise sound and MIDI generating programs may have to recompute this.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"By default, a dalsegno or dacapo attribute indicates that the jump should occur the first time through, while","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"a tocoda attribute indicates the jump should occur the second time through. The time that jumps occur can be","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"changed by using the time-only attribute.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The forward-repeat attribute indicates that a forward repeat sign is implied but not displayed. It is used for","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"example in two-part forms with repeats, such as a minuet and trio where no repeat is displayed at the start of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the trio. This usually occurs after a barline. When used it always has the value of “yes”.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The fine attribute follows the final note or rest in a movement with a da capo or dal segno direction. If","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"numeric, the value represents the actual duration of the final note or rest, which can be ambiguous in written","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"notation and different among parts and voices. The value may also be “yes” to indicate no change to the final","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"duration.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"If the sound element applies only particular times through a repeat, the time-only attribute indicates which","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"times to apply the sound element.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Pizzicato in a sound element effects all following notes. Yes indicates pizzicato, no indicates arco.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The pan and elevation attributes are deprecated in Version 2.0. The pan and elevation elements in the midi-","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"instrument element should be used instead. The meaning of the pan and elevation attributes is the same as for","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the pan and elevation elements. If both are present, the mid-instrument elements take priority.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The damper-pedal, soft-pedal, and sostenuto-pedal attributes effect playback of the three common piano pedals","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"and their MIDI controller equivalents. The yes value indicates the pedal is depressed; no indicates the pedal","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"is released. A numeric value from 0 to 100 may also be used for half pedaling. This value is the percentage","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"that the pedal is depressed. A value of 0 is equivalent to no, and a value of 100 is equivalent to yes.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Instrument changes, MIDI devices, MIDI instruments, and playback techniques are changed using the instrument-","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"change, midi-device, midi-instrument, and play elements. When there are multiple instances of these elements,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"they should be grouped together by instrument using the id attribute values.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The offset element is used to indicate that the sound takes place offset from the current score position. If","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the sound element is a child of a direction element, the sound offset element overrides the direction offset","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"element if both elements are present. Note that the offset reflects the intended musical position for the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"change in sound. It should not be used to compensate for latency issues in particular hardware configurations.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Maps the MusicXML ","type":"text"},{"code":"<sound>","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" element.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"metadata":{"title":"MXLSound","modules":[{"name":"IvorMXL"}],"externalID":"s:7IvorMXL8MXLSoundV","roleHeading":"Structure","role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MXLSound","kind":"identifier"}],"symbolKind":"struct","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLSound","kind":"identifier"}]},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL"]]},"topicSections":[{"generated":true,"title":"Structures","identifiers":["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLSound\/Group-swift.struct"],"anchor":"Structures"},{"generated":true,"title":"Initializers","identifiers":["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLSound\/init(id:group:swing:offset:tempo:dynamics:isDaCapo:segno:dalsegno:coda:tocoda:divisions:impliesForwardRepeat:fine:timeOnly:isPizzicato:pan:elevation:damperPedal:softPedal:sostenutoPedal:)"],"anchor":"Initializers"},{"generated":true,"title":"Instance Properties","identifiers":["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLSound\/coda","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLSound\/dalsegno","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLSound\/damperPedal","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLSound\/divisions","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLSound\/dynamics","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLSound\/elevation","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLSound\/fine","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLSound\/group-swift.property","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLSound\/id","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLSound\/impliesForwardRepeat","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLSound\/isDaCapo","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLSound\/isPizzicato","doc:\/\/